## Cause

The issue stems from how `this` is handled. In a regular function, `this` doesn't point to the `Timer` object. When `setInterval` invokes the function, `this` instead refers to the global object (or `undefined` in strict mode).

Because of this, `this.seconds` doesn't access the `seconds` property of your `Timer` instance—it's `undefined` instead. When you add `1` to `undefined`, the result is `NaN`, which then gets printed to the console every second.

