  const res = await fetch(`/api/users/${userId}`);
  if (!res.ok) {
    throw new Error(`Failed to load profile: ${res.status}`);
  }
  const data = await res.json();
  return data.name.toUpperCase();
}
```

This version waits for the fetch to complete, checks that the response is valid, and returns the correct value.

## Cause

The function inside `setInterval` is a normal function. Therefore its `this` does not point to the instance of `Timer`. Instead, `this` points to the global object.

The global object does not have a `seconds` property. Therefore `this.seconds += 1` produces `undefined + 1`, which is `NaN`.

## Solution

Use an arrow function. An arrow function does not have its own `this`. It uses `this` from the surrounding scope, which is the instance of `Timer`.

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
  }
  start() {
    setInterval(() => {
      this.seconds += 1;
      console.log(this.seconds);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
new Timer().start();
```
